@meta
id: 12-12
title: Change everything up to a landmark
chapter: 12
type: lesson
skills: search-motion
par: 44
minutes: 6
vscode: select from here to there with the mouse and retype
open: refund.service.ts

@brief
Partial refunds are refunding the whole order. The gateway call in
`submit` still passes the order total and the old `'LEGACY'` flag:

    await this.gatewayClient.refund(order.id, order.total, 'LEGACY');

It should pass the refund's own amount, and the flag is gone from the
gateway API:

    await this.gatewayClient.refund(order.id, refund.amount);

The interesting part is the shape of the edit, not the code. You want to
replace a run of text that starts at a word you can name and ends at a
character you can name, and neither end is a word, a line or a bracket.
No text object covers it. A search does.

@keys
| `c/text` `Enter` | change from here up to the next match, then type |
| `d/text` `Enter` | the delete version |
| `y/text` `Enter` | the yank version |
| `gU/text` `Enter` | and so on — every operator takes it |

@hints
Get the cursor to where the replacement starts, then let one operator
cover everything between there and the closing bracket.
--
`c` takes a motion and a search is a motion, so `c/` followed by the
character you want to stop before will do it. The closing parenthesis is
a fine thing to search for.
--
`/order.total` `Enter` lands you at the start of the text to replace.
Then `c/)` `Enter` clears everything up to the closing bracket and leaves
you in insert mode. Type the new argument and press `Esc`.

@solution
/order.total<Enter>  c/)<Enter>  refund.amount<Esc>  :wq<Enter>

Thirty-five keystrokes, fourteen of which are the new argument.

Notice what the search motion is doing for you here. The text you wanted
to replace was `order.total, 'LEGACY'` — a fragment with a comma, a
space, a string literal and no structure to grab hold of. Selecting it by
hand means aiming twice. Naming its end — "up to the closing bracket" —
means aiming once, and you do not have to be accurate about the middle.

`c/` and `d/` are the two you will reach for constantly. `y/` is the same
idea for copying: `y/}` `Enter` grabs everything from here to the next
closing brace, which is a quick way to lift a block out of a file. All
the operators take a search, including the ones that do not feel like
they should: `>/text` indents up to a match, `gU/text` upper-cases up to
one.

One thing to watch. Because `/` motions are exclusive, `c/)` stops just
before the bracket and leaves it in place — which is why the code still
compiles. If you had wanted to swallow the bracket too, you would need
the next lesson.

@start refund.service.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { GatewayClient } from './gateway.client';
import { AuditProducer } from '../events/audit.producer';

@injectable()
export class RefundService {
  constructor(
    @inject(GatewayClient) protected gatewayClient: GatewayClient,
    @inject(AuditProducer) protected auditProducer: AuditProducer,
  ) {}

  public async authorise(args: { orderId: string; amount: number }) {
    const authorisation = await this.gatewayClient.authorise({
      orderId: args.orderId,
      amount: args.amount,
    });

    if (!authorisation.approved) {
      throw exception('REFUND_NOT_AUTHORISED');
    }

    return authorisation;
  }

  public async submit(refund: { id: string; orderId: string; amount: number }) {
    const order = await this.gatewayClient.lookup(refund.orderId);

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    await this.gatewayClient.refund(order.id, order.total, 'LEGACY');

    await this.auditProducer.publish({
      type: 'refund.submitted',
      refundId: refund.id,
      orderId: order.id,
    });

    return { refundId: refund.id, submitted: true };
  }

  public async status(args: { refundId: string }) {
    return this.gatewayClient.refundStatus(args.refundId);
  }
}

@expected refund.service.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { GatewayClient } from './gateway.client';
import { AuditProducer } from '../events/audit.producer';

@injectable()
export class RefundService {
  constructor(
    @inject(GatewayClient) protected gatewayClient: GatewayClient,
    @inject(AuditProducer) protected auditProducer: AuditProducer,
  ) {}

  public async authorise(args: { orderId: string; amount: number }) {
    const authorisation = await this.gatewayClient.authorise({
      orderId: args.orderId,
      amount: args.amount,
    });

    if (!authorisation.approved) {
      throw exception('REFUND_NOT_AUTHORISED');
    }

    return authorisation;
  }

  public async submit(refund: { id: string; orderId: string; amount: number }) {
    const order = await this.gatewayClient.lookup(refund.orderId);

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    await this.gatewayClient.refund(order.id, refund.amount);

    await this.auditProducer.publish({
      type: 'refund.submitted',
      refundId: refund.id,
      orderId: order.id,
    });

    return { refundId: refund.id, submitted: true };
  }

  public async status(args: { refundId: string }) {
    return this.gatewayClient.refundStatus(args.refundId);
  }
}
